%[text] %[text:anchor:T_TPPFC] # Design and Analyze a Totem-Pole Boost PFC Converter
%[text] A totem-pole boost power factor correction (PFC) converter is a bridgeless single-phase topology with one line-frequency leg and one high-frequency leg. This design solution shows how to size the power stage and select the fast-leg and slow-leg semiconductors. The model uses the totem-pole boost PFC plant and controller blocks from `ConverterLibrary`. You then simulate the closed loop, plot the AC and DC waveforms, analyze the input-current harmonics, and estimate power loss and efficiency.
%[text:tableOfContents]{"heading":"Table of Contents"}
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_scope] ## Design Solution Scope
%[text] In this design solution, you:
%[text] - Size the power stage and review the estimated design parameters
%[text] - Select fast-leg and slow-leg semiconductors that meet the required ratings
%[text] - Simulate the closed loop and inspect the AC input and DC output waveforms
%[text] - Analyze the switching ripple and the line-frequency current harmonics (THD)
%[text] - Estimate power loss and efficiency at a specified device junction temperature \
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_sys] ## System Overview
%[text] The model comprises the Totem-Pole Boost PFC plant and controller. The bridgeless totem-pole topology switches a MOSFET fast leg at high frequency. It commutates a slow leg (diode, MOSFET, or thyristor) at the line frequency, which removes the input rectifier's two diode drops.
%[text] The controller regulates the DC bus with an outer voltage loop that trims a power reference. An inner current loop shapes the boost-inductor current into a rectified sine wave locked to the line, which gives unity power factor.
modelName = "TotemPoleBoostPFCConverter";
open_system(modelName);
%[text] ![](text:image:3aac)
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_design] ## Select Totem-Pole Boost PFC Converter Parameters
%[text] To size the power stage, use the `EstimateTotemPoleBoostPFCParameters` live script. You specify the design requirements. It returns the boost inductance, the DC-bus capacitance and the required device rating, checked across the line and load range.
%[text] To open the parameter estimation workflow, enter this command in the MATLAB® Command Window:
%[text] ```matlabCodeExample
%[text] edit 'EstimateTotemPoleBoostPFCParameters'
%[text] ```
%[text] Load the default parameters:
if ~exist('totemPoleDesign','var')
    TotemPoleBoostPFCDefaultInputParameters;
end
disp(table(designTable,'VariableNames',"Totem-Pole Boost PFC Estimated Design Parameters")); %[output:5cb37d6d]
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_selectcomp] ## Select Converter Components
%[text] This design solution helps you rate the fast-leg and slow-leg semiconductors and pick catalog parts that meet those ratings. The parts come from the Simscape™ parts feature. In the workflow, you choose whether the slow leg is a diode, a MOSFET, or a thyristor.
%[text] To open the component selection workflow, enter:
%[text] ```matlabCodeExample
%[text] edit 'SelectTotemPoleBoostPFCComponents'
%[text] ```
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_op] ## Define the Operating Point
%[text] Set the DC bus voltage and the load power for the waveform, harmonic and loss analyses.
%[text] The waveform run covers ten line cycles at a step fine enough to resolve the switching ripple, so the harmonic analysis reuses that run.
voltageReference = simscape.Value(totemPoleDesign.outputVoltage.value, "V");   % DC bus setpoint (Vref)
outputLoadPower  = simscape.Value(0.5*totemPoleDesign.ratedPower.value, "W");  % Output power (Pload)
simulationTime   = simscape.Value(60e-3, "s");                                 % Stop time of the loss run, three line cycles at 50 Hz
waveformSimulationTime = simscape.Value(200e-3, "s");                          % Stop time of the waveform run, ten line cycles at 50 Hz
rippleSpanMultiple = 10;                                                       % The ripple spectrum spans DC to this multiple of the switching frequency
maximumSolverStep  = 1/(2*rippleSpanMultiple*switchingFrequency.value);         % Solver step (s) that resolves that span
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_plot] ### Plot Simulation Result
%[text] To plot the converter waveforms, use `getConverterWaveforms`. It simulates the plant's `Simplified` variant. `FocusCycles` sets how many line cycles at the end of the simulation are plotted.
[totemPoleBoostPFCIVData, totemPoleBoostPFCSimulationLog] = getConverterWaveforms(modelName, Converter="TotemPoleBoostPFC", ... %[output:group:884f9eee] %[output:894fa081]
    Vref=voltageReference, Pload=outputLoadPower, ... %[output:894fa081]
    SimTime=waveformSimulationTime, FrequencyHz=lineFrequency.value, FocusCycles=2, ... %[output:894fa081]
    MaxStep=maximumSolverStep); %[output:group:884f9eee] %[output:894fa081]
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_harm] ### Harmonic Analysis and THD
%[text] To analyze the AC input current, use `estimateConverterHarmonics`. It reads the log of the waveform run above and plots:
%[text] - the broadband switching-ripple spectrum up to `RippleSpanMultiple` times the switching frequency
%[text] - the line-frequency harmonic stem through the 10th, THD in its title, then a THD-by-standard table (IEEE 519, IEC 61000). \
harmonicData = estimateConverterHarmonics(totemPoleBoostPFCSimulationLog, Converter="TotemPoleBoostPFC", ... %[output:group:fddfd2cf] %[output:3facbb8a] %[output:012fa760] %[output:9801b15d]
    FundamentalHz=lineFrequency.value, SwitchingHz=switchingFrequency.value, ... %[output:3facbb8a] %[output:012fa760] %[output:9801b15d]
    RippleSpanMultiple=rippleSpanMultiple, ... %[output:3facbb8a] %[output:012fa760] %[output:9801b15d]
    ShowHarmonicTable=false); %[output:group:fddfd2cf] %[output:3facbb8a] %[output:012fa760] %[output:9801b15d]
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_loss] ### Estimate Power Loss and Efficiency
%[text] To estimate the switching and conduction losses and the efficiency, use `getConverterPowerLoss`. It simulates the plant's `Detailed` variant at the junction temperature you set.
%[text] A PFC's loss is averaged over whole line cycles. `MinCycles` sets how many line cycles at the end of the run are averaged; set `simulationTime` so the converter reaches steady state before them.
simulationTemperature = simscape.Value(25,"degC");   % Device junction temperature for the loss estimation
lossData = getConverterPowerLoss(modelName, Converter="TotemPoleBoostPFC", ... %[output:group:0d035888] %[output:34fbbae6] %[output:1ef519f9]
    Vref=voltageReference, Pload=outputLoadPower, ... %[output:34fbbae6] %[output:1ef519f9]
    SimTime=simulationTime, SwitchingLossDetail="lumped", ... %[output:34fbbae6] %[output:1ef519f9]
    SimulationTemperature=simulationTemperature, ... %[output:34fbbae6] %[output:1ef519f9]
    FrequencyHz=lineFrequency.value, MinCycles=1); %[output:group:0d035888] %[output:34fbbae6] %[output:1ef519f9]
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_concl] ## Conclusion
%[text] This design solution shows how to use MATLAB scripting and Simscape Electrical™ to design a totem-pole boost PFC converter. It sizes the power stage, selects the semiconductors and simulates waveforms, harmonics and efficiency.
